{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLe6Ppn86pqt6jpCg9Fc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: `RidgeClassifierCV` now accepts `store_cv_values`, and with `store_cv_values=True` it keeps the cross-validation values in `cv_values_`. The whole change is in `sklearn/linear_model/ridge.py`:\n\n- **Constructor:** `__init__` takes `store_cv_values=False` and passes it to the parent class. The parent already knew how to compute and store the values; the classifier just never passed the flag on.\n- **Docs:** I added the parameter to the docstring. I also corrected the `cv_values_` shape to `[n_samples, n_targets, n_alphas]`, because for this classifier the array always has a middle dimension, even with only two classes.\n\n**Testing:** the issue's example now runs. With 3 classes, `cv_values_` has shape `(100, 3, n_alphas)`; with 2 classes it is `(100, 1, n_alphas)`. The existing tests in `sklearn/linear_model/tests/test_ridge.py` pass (35 passed).\n\nOne separate thing came up while testing: if `alphas` is given as plain integers (e.g. `[1, 2]`), `fit` fails with `ValueError: Integers to negative integer powers are not allowed`. The problem is in the existing cross-validation code, not in this change, so I left it alone. Passing floats like `[1.0, 2.0]` works."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1590,"cache_read_input_tokens":22322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1590},"output_tokens":456,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2b0e5cb-c4bb-4b04-beb5-798af29ec09c","uuid":"6bd62003-a548-4f2e-8fed-f3dbab83facd","timestamp":"2026-11-23T22:22:08.689Z","request_id":"req_011ChLe6PhLao54dAt2fyDx3"}
